### F2 — Outlier label literals `{-1, -2}` hard-coded in `_weighted_cluster_center` instead of `_OUTLIER_ENCODING`
severity: medium
evidence: sklearn/cluster/_hdbscan/hdbscan.py:895 — `n_clusters = len(set(self.labels_) - {-1, -2})`. The module already single-sources these labels in `_OUTLIER_ENCODING` (lines 65-79) which the rest of the file references via `_OUTLIER_ENCODING["infinite"]["label"]` / `["missing"]["label"]` (lines 842, 843, 848, 850, 964, 965, 968, 969), and the test suite even builds its own `OUTLIER_SET` from it (test_hdbscan.py:43). This literal set is out of sync with `_OUTLIER_ENCODING`: it silently omits `-3` (the "missing" label), causing `n_clusters` to over-count when any `missing` label survives into `self.labels_`.
scenario: "User fits HDBSCAN on data containing `np.nan` rows with `store_centers='centroid'` → missing samples receive label `-3` in `self.labels_` → `_weighted_cluster_center` computes `n_clusters = len(set(labels_) - {-1, -2})` which still contains `-3` → `centroids_` is over-allocated by one row, and the loop `range(n_clusters)` iterates one extra `idx` for which no points match, yielding a NaN centroid row from `np.average` over an empty slice."
contract: Derive the outlier label set from `_OUTLIER_ENCODING` in exactly one place (e.g. `_OUTLIER_LABELS = {-1} | {v["label"] for v in _OUTLIER_ENCODING.values()}`) and subtract that set here.
